CONDUCTION Lexicon
Source: "Butch Morris and the Art of Conduction" (Stanley, 2009, PhD dissertation)

WHAT CONDUCTION IS
Conduction (Conducted Improvisation/Interpretation) is a system developed by Lawrence D. "Butch" Morris beginning in 1985. It is a patented vocabulary of ideographic signs and gestures used to modify or construct a real-time musical arrangement or composition. Each sign and gesture transmits generative information for interpretation by the individual and the collective, providing instantaneous possibilities for altering or initiating harmony, melody, rhythm, articulation, phrasing, or form. Morris distinguishes Conduction (from silence or free material) from Induction (applying the vocabulary to a printed score).

Conduction is best understood not as a method but as a new musical instrument — with the conductor's body as hardware and the gesture vocabulary as software. The conductor's moving body becomes a real-time agent of complexly ordered sound. The ensemble functions as an aggregate of compositional nodes.

CORE PRINCIPLES
The conductor composes and performs simultaneously. There is no pre-composed material. What a Conduction will be is only ascertainable after it has become.
The authority of the printed score migrates from static page to moving body.
Players can never lose sight of the conductor — not even momentarily. This is non-negotiable.
The macrostructural cues offered by the conductor are themselves an improvisation. Both conductor and ensemble are in a constant state of symbiotic tension.
Pre-performance rehearsals develop facility within the Conduction system — they are not for learning material to be performed. Nothing is rehearsed in advance.
Conduction is primarily a live performance experience. Something fundamental is lost on recording because the visible tensions and risks of the methodology are obscured.
Morris describes his role as environmental orchestration — crafting assemblages from sonic materials.

HISTORICAL CONTEXT
First Conduction: February 1, 1985. The Kitchen, New York. "Current Trends in Racism in Modern America — A Work in Progress."
Over 70 numbered Conductions documented in the chronology through 1996.
Morris has applied his vocabulary to over 5,000 musicians globally across a vast array of traditions — Western classical, jazz, non-Western, electronic, vocal.
Notable ensembles: NuBlu Orchestra, Chorus of Poets, Skyscraper (various city editions), Eva Kant, David Murray Big Band.
Testament (1995) — 10 CD box set documenting the first 50 Conductions.

RELATIONSHIP TO IMPROVISATION AND COMPOSITION
Conduction is not free jazz, not traditional conducting, and not structured improvisation in the conventional sense.
Morris' core question: "Why preserve the differences between notation and improvisation?"
Conduction distributes compositional authorship across the ensemble. The conductor determines macrostructure. Individual players fill directives with creative responses conditioned by their own musical evaluation.
The music produced is not repeatable. Each Conduction exists only as it unfolds.

PERFORMANCE REQUIREMENTS FOR PLAYERS
Players must maintain continuous visual contact with the conductor throughout the performance. This is a non-negotiable requirement distinct from all other ensemble traditions.
Players must be fully invested in the sonic environment — listening to all other players, not only their own line.
Musical decisions must be aesthetic and valenced — every individual contribution enters an ongoing communal discussion of musical values.
The conductor's macrostructural cues are themselves an improvisation. Players respond to a score that is being written in real time.

MORRIS ON THE CONDUCTOR'S ROLE
"The conductor's job is not to channel the spirit of a composer from a printed score. It is to make music — in real time, with the ensemble."
Morris has been critical of the limited creative role of conducting in the Western orchestral tradition — conductors are powerful but powerless to actually make music.
Conduction collapses the roles of conductor and composer into a single job description.
Morris uses facial expression, mouthed commentary, and verbal cues external to the formal vocabulary to humanize the system and fill gaps in real time. This is not scripted — it is warm social context.

SOUNDPAINTING CROSS-REFERENCE
Morris acknowledges Walter Thompson's Soundpainting as a related gestural system. Both operate from a conductor-as-real-time-composer model. Key distinctions: Conduction's vocabulary was developed specifically for jazz and improvised ensemble contexts; Soundpainting has a broader scope including theatrical and interdisciplinary applications. Both systems share the pedagogy of a learned gesture vocabulary applied to live ensemble creation.

THE VOCABULARY (Appendix II — provided and formatted by Lawrence D. "Butch" Morris)

1. SUSTAIN (Fermata or Held Sound)
Sign: Left hand palm up, arm extended toward musicians.
Meaning: One continuous sound. Sound may change each time a downbeat is given.

2. DOWNBEAT
Gesture: Downward stroke of hand or baton.
Meaning: Used to begin, commence, and/or activate a directive. Given bigger than all preparatory information.

3. REPEAT
Sign: Left hand forming the letter U.
Meaning: To capture (emulate, imitate, or follow) information as close as possible, or to vamp/riff.
Three circumstances: (1) If not playing — musician constructs information then repeats it. (2) If playing — repeat sign followed by downbeat captures previously played information. (3) To capture information from any source — point toward sound, then downbeat.

4. ACCOMPANY
Sign: Right hand pointing at musician, left hand index finger pointing to ear then to musician/information to be accompanied.
Meaning: To support and assist particular activity. Gravitational — no downbeat used.

5. IMAGING/SHAPING
Sign: Left hand pointing to chest, right hand pointing at musician.
Meaning: To elaborate, articulate, shape, develop, and/or create variations in real time on existing information. Conductor must first establish a visual representation of ensemble information then reshape it through gestural suggestion. Musician returns to original idea with Reconstruct or Go Back sign.

6A-6B. DYNAMICS (Loud/Soft — FFF to PPP)
Two methods:
(1) Gesture: Palms facing floor, raise for louder, lower for softer. No downbeat. Immediate response.
(2) Sign: Clenched fist in chest area for loud. Index finger to lips for soft. Downbeat follows.

7. MEMORY
Sign: Left hand to forehead, followed by numerical designation (1 finger = memory 1, etc.).
Meaning: Recall and re-evaluation of specific information. Whatever musician is playing when designation is given is what they return to when designation is repeated with downbeat.

8. PREPARE (Pay Attention)
Sign: Left hand palm facing musician(s).
Meaning: Musician(s) about to receive preparatory information. If playing — continue but await new information, change on downbeat. If not playing — give full attention. Given before graphic or rhythmic information and when multiple directives are being given.

9A-9B. CHANGE IN TONALITY
Sign: Left hand thumb up (higher) or thumb down (lower).
Meaning: Transpose, transfer, shift, or harmodulate what musician is playing up or down on downbeat. Gradual move achieved by turning thumb slowly without downbeat.

10. TEMPO (Pulse or Meter)
Gesture: Right hand or baton in chest area beating desired pulse. Left hand beats "1" when distinguishing between 2, 3, 4 beats per measure. Followed by downbeat.

11. PROPORTIONAL SPEED
Sign: Fingers of left hand pointing to floor.
1 finger (index) = SLOW
2 fingers (index and middle) = MEDIUM
3 fingers (index, middle, third) = FAST
Meaning: Immediate relative change of speed.

12. RHYTHM
Gesture: Right arm extended toward ensemble. Right hand taps desired rhythmic idea while left hand gives "1" or the beginning of the rhythm.
Meaning: Replicate or create a rhythm pattern.

13. DEVELOP
Gesture: Hands palm to palm facing left and right at chest level, separating and returning.
Meaning: Variate, elaborate, embellish, transform, augment, diminish, fragment, deconstruct, or reconstruct a specific point of information. Palms together = the original idea. Hands separating = development. Hands returning = reconstruction. Degree of development determined by space between hands.

13A. DEVELOP (Round/Canon)
Gesture: Hands palm to palm facing up and down at chest level, spreading up and down.
Meaning: Musician maintains rhythmic structure while varying melodic/harmonic content. Each musician develops their own information in their own time.

14. ENTRY (Pedestrian)
Gesture: Wave of left hand, beckoning.
Meaning: Designates a pedestrian — temporarily free of all ensemble directives. May contribute or not. Primary duty is to qualify ensemble information as a major contributor. Gravitational — no downbeat. Resumes place in ensemble when given overruling directive.

15. FEATURE (Solo)
Gesture: Pointing to musician, left hand index finger beckoning.
Meaning: Designates a featured musician (soloist). Temporarily free of all ensemble directives. Quantifies ensemble information. Gravitational — no downbeat. Resumes place when given overruling directive.

16. LITERAL MOVEMENT
Sign: Baton parallel to body, in front of face.
Meaning: Real-time, mid-air graphic information. Range of baton indicates sonic register — lower baton = lower sound, higher baton = higher sound. Each musician with this directive reads and interprets baton movement on their instrument. Downbeat is first gesture made after sign is given.

17. PANORAMA
Sign: Baton upside-down, parallel to body.
Meaning: Stop then begin, or begin then stop a contribution.
If not contributing — contribute only when baton is in their physical field.
If contributing — stop only when baton is in their field, then resume.
Downbeat is first movement of baton following the description.

18. PEDAL
Sign: Left hand palm down in front of chest.
Meaning: Attack and decay/diminuendo.

19. ARTICULATION
Sign: Right hand or baton parallel to body over right shoulder giving small preliminary instruction.
Meaning: Indicates how sounds are to be enunciated — short or long. Followed by larger gestures satisfying a downbeat requirement.
